sections.text:
The commonest mistake is treating environmental management as opinion. Claims need evidence: a measurement, a standard, a law, a study.

Others are:
confusing pollution concentration with total load;
quoting a limit or a law from another country, or from an old version;
confusing mitigation, adaptation and compensation;
assuming recycling solves waste, when reduction comes first in the waste hierarchy;
ignoring the people affected, especially poor or minority communities;
writing an impact assessment with no baseline data and no monitoring plan.

Be honest about uncertainty. Saying a figure varies, and naming where to check it, is better than inventing a number.
